      <t:profileDesc xmlns:t="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
         <t:langUsage><t:language ident="en">English</t:language><t:language ident="am">Amharic</t:language><t:language ident="gez">Gǝʿǝz</t:language></t:langUsage>
         <t:abstract>
            <t:p>This is a general record of any work teaching the Orthodox faith and concerning the five mysteries: the mystery of the Trinity, the Incarnation, the Baptism, the Eucharist, and the Resurrection of the Dead.
               At a cursory survey, it seems that the most widespread version is that contained in manuscripts such as <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/ESddm013">Gāntā ʾAfašum, Danderā Dabra Mǝḥrat Mikāʾel, DDM-013</t:ref>, <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML379">Collegeville, Hill Museum and Manuscript Library, MS EMML no. 379</t:ref>, 
               <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML4404">Collegeville, Hill Museum and Manuscript Library, MS EMML no. 4404</t:ref>, and <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML4963">Collegeville, Hill Museum and Manuscript Library, MS EMML no. 4963</t:ref>.
               The texts vary in length, from very concise ones such as <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML3247">Collegeville, Hill Museum and Manuscript Library, MS EMML no. 3247</t:ref> and <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML3517">Collegeville, Hill Museum and Manuscript Library, MS EMML no. 3517</t:ref>, which contain only four folia, to those of considerable length, 
               with over one hundred folia, such as <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML3408">Collegeville, Hill Museum and Manuscript Library, MS EMML no. 3408</t:ref>.
               The text in <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMDA206">Dabra Mārqos, Qarānǝyo Madḫāne ʿĀlam, G1-IV-310</t:ref>, dated to 1792, is atypical in that a relatively large part of the text consists of Gǝʿǝz quotations, whereas Amharic narration is present to a lesser extent.
               The text in <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML1288">Collegeville, Hill Museum and Manuscript Library, MS EMML no. 1288</t:ref> is peculiar in that the mysteries of the faith are consistently presented in a dialogue of questions and answers, which is also reflected in the layout.
               Some texts begin with an introduction, which may or may not be part of the ʾAmmǝstu ʾaʿmāda mǝśṭir. 
               In this introduction, the whole text is called “Maṣḥafa mǝgbār wa-hāymānot” (e.g., <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML 5048"><seg corresp="EMML 5048">No item: EMML 5048</seg></t:ref>, <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML5594">Addis ʿAläm, ʾAddis ʿĀlam Māryām, MS EMML no. 5594</t:ref>).
               In <t:ref type="mss" corresp="https://betamasaheft.eu/EMML5594">Addis ʿAläm, ʾAddis ʿĀlam Māryām, MS EMML no. 5594</t:ref>, Maṣḥafa mǝgbār wa-hāymānot is separated from the ʾAmmǝstu ʾaʿmāda mǝśṭir by another text.
            </t:p>
         </t:abstract>
        
